{"qid":"Q419","names":["Republic of Peru"],"title":"Republic of Peru","endonym":{"native":"República del Perú","lang":"es"},"capsule":"The Republic of Peru declared independence from Spain on 28 July 1821 under the proclamation of José de San Martín, with formal military liberation completed by Simón Bolívar's victory at Ayacucho on 9 December 1824. Throughout the 19th century Peru experienced chronic political instability interrupted by the profitable guano boom (1840s–1870s), which funded state consolidation; the War of the Pacific (1879–1884) against Chile and Bolivia resulted in the loss of Tarapacá and the temporary Chilean occupation of Lima. The 20th century brought alternating civilian and military governments, including the twelve-year military reformist government of Juan Velasco Alvarado (1968–1975), and a protracted internal conflict with the Shining Path insurgency (1980–2000) before consolidation of electoral democracy.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. National boundaries are well defined by post-war treaties (Treaty of Ancón 1883, Ecuadorian border fixed 1942–1998); the dataset's attestation from 1822 onward reflects treaty-derived boundaries. Some Amazonian frontier demarcation remained approximate into the 20th century.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Peru","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Peru"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Peru","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peru"}],"britannica":"place/Peru","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Peru","rulers":[{"qid":"Q134160","name":"José de San Martín","start":1821,"end":1822,"image":"José de San Martín (retrato, c.1828).jpg","imageUrl"